WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.639
Multiple agents working together can beat a single agent, but they're not free.

2
00:00:04.639 --> 00:00:09.279
They burn tokens fast and add coordination you have to manage and pay for.

3
00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:13.199
This lesson walks through the token economics, the latency tax,

4
00:00:13.199 --> 00:00:19.760
and a simple fit rule for deciding when a multi-agent design is actually worth reaching for. Let's dive in.

5
00:00:20.959 --> 00:00:24.799
Start with the shape of the thing, because the shape explains the cost.

6
00:00:24.799 --> 00:00:27.600
A single agent is one model running in a loop.

7
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.360
It calls tools, thinks, and holds the whole task inside one context window.

8
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.799
An orchestrator is a lead agent that plans the work,

9
00:00:36.799 --> 00:00:41.439
spawns sub-agents to do pieces of it, and later combines what they return.

10
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.759
A sub-agent is a worker.

11
00:00:43.759 --> 00:00:48.720
It gets a scoped slice of the task and runs in its own separate context,

12
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.200
with its own prompt and its own reasoning.

13
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.479
So, moving from one agent to many really means adding an orchestrator on top

14
00:00:56.479 --> 00:00:58.639
and several independent workers underneath.

15
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.559
Hold on to that picture, because it explains everything about the cost we are about to look at.

16
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.040
Here's the heart of it.

17
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.160
The core cost driver is beautifully simple.

18
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:15.199
More agents means more model calls, and more model calls means more tokens.

19
00:01:15.199 --> 00:01:19.360
Anthropic put rough numbers on this from their own research system.

20
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.000
Think of a plain chat turn as the baseline.

21
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.440
Call it one times.

22
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:29.919
A single agent running in a loop with tools uses roughly four times the tokens of that chat baseline.

23
00:01:29.919 --> 00:01:35.040
And a multi-agent system uses about 15 times the chat baseline.

24
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.480
So, multi-agent isn't a little more expensive.

25
00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.279
It is several times more expensive than even a single agent. Why?

26
00:01:43.279 --> 00:01:48.400
Because every sub-agent you spawn carries its own prompt and does its own reasoning.

27
00:01:48.959 --> 00:01:52.959
Those costs don't overlap, they stack, and they add up fast.

28
00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.239
Let's break down where that spend actually comes from, because it isn't one thing.

29
00:01:58.239 --> 00:02:00.239
First, orchestrator overhead.

30
00:02:00.239 --> 00:02:06.319
The lead agent spends tokens planning the job, deciding how to split it, and delegating.

31
00:02:06.319 --> 00:02:08.160
All before any real work happens.

32
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.960
Second, parallel fan-out.

33
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:17.279
Each sub-agent repeats a chunk of setup context and reasons through its slice independently.

34
00:02:17.279 --> 00:02:21.520
So the more workers you launch, the more that base cost multiplies.

35
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.240
Third, result stitching.

36
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:30.960
Once the sub-agents finish, the orchestrator has to read their outputs and merge them into one coherent answer.

37
00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.199
And that is yet another model pass.

38
00:02:33.839 --> 00:02:36.479
Planning, fan-out, merging.

39
00:02:36.479 --> 00:02:42.399
Three stages, each one paying tokens, and together they explain that 15 times figure.

40
00:02:43.679 --> 00:02:47.440
Cost is the headline, but it isn't the only tax you pay.

41
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.119
Splitting work across agents also makes the system harder to run well.

42
00:02:51.679 --> 00:02:55.199
The orchestrator has to decompose the task cleanly up front.

43
00:02:55.199 --> 00:02:58.160
And clean decomposition is genuinely hard.

44
00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.360
If the split is messy, sub-agents can drift off course or duplicate each other's effort.

45
00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.199
And you pay for that wasted work.

46
00:03:05.839 --> 00:03:09.919
Then, because pieces run in parallel and finish at different times,

47
00:03:09.919 --> 00:03:13.919
you have to gather and reconcile their results before you can answer.

48
00:03:13.919 --> 00:03:16.080
All of that adds latency and moving parts.

49
00:03:16.639 --> 00:03:18.399
The honest framing is this.

50
00:03:18.399 --> 00:03:22.720
You are trading latency and complexity for a shot at a better final result.

51
00:03:23.279 --> 00:03:25.119
Sometimes that trade pays off.

52
00:03:25.119 --> 00:03:25.919
Often it doesn't.

53
00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:28.639
So when does it pay off?

54
00:03:28.639 --> 00:03:30.399
It comes down to the shape of the work.

55
00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.479
Multi-agent is a good fit for breadth-first tasks.

56
00:03:34.479 --> 00:03:38.960
Work you can split into independent directions that explore in parallel.

57
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.960
Researching many sources at once is the classic example.

58
00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.960
Each thread barely touches the others.

59
00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.520
So parallel workers genuinely help.

60
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.000
It is a poor fit for tightly coupled work,

61
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.000
where agents share one context and each step depends on the last.

62
00:03:54.639 --> 00:03:58.240
Splitting that just means constantly sinking state between workers.

63
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.880
And a single agent holding everything stays cleaner, faster, and cheaper.

64
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.360
Coding is the trap here.

65
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.960
A code base looks big, so people assume it parallelizes.

66
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.960
But most coding tasks have far fewer truly parallel pieces than research does.

67
00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.640
Files depend on each other.

68
00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.559
And changes have to stay consistent.

69
00:04:19.119 --> 00:04:21.519
Size isn't the same as parallelism.

70
00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:25.760
Let's turn that into a rule you can actually apply.

71
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.799
Before you reach for multiple agents, ask three questions.

72
00:04:28.799 --> 00:04:33.359
One, can the work split into parallel directions that barely touch each other?

73
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:40.239
Two, does each piece fit in its own context without needing shared state passed back and forth?

74
00:04:40.239 --> 00:04:44.239
And three, is the task valuable enough to justify a token bill

75
00:04:44.239 --> 00:04:46.399
several times higher than a single agent?

76
00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.040
If you answer yes to all three, multi-agent has a real shot at earning its cost.

77
00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.519
If you answer no to even one of them, a single agent is almost always the better call.

78
00:04:58.079 --> 00:04:59.200
Notice the framing.

79
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.040
The default is one agent.

80
00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.959
And multi-agent is the thing you justify, not the thing you assume.

81
00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:08.640
When multi-agent genuinely fits,

82
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.720
you can soften the cost by matching models to roles

83
00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.079
instead of using your most expensive model everywhere.

84
00:05:16.079 --> 00:05:19.920
Your strongest, most capable model makes sense as the orchestrator

85
00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.040
because planning and merging need the best judgment.

86
00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.359
Then lighter, faster models can handle the scoped sub-tasks

87
00:05:27.359 --> 00:05:30.160
where the job is narrow and well-defined.

88
00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.399
Check the current model documentation for which tiers those are today

89
00:05:34.399 --> 00:05:36.160
because the lineup shifts.

90
00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.959
And you reserve the whole pattern for high-value tasks that repay the spend.

91
00:05:40.959 --> 00:05:43.679
The point I want to leave you with is intent.

92
00:05:43.679 --> 00:05:46.720
The goal is buying real, measurable performance.

93
00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:50.880
It is never adding agents just because the architecture looks impressive on a diagram.

94
00:05:52.399 --> 00:05:54.720
Running agents in parallel buys you breadth.

95
00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.559
You cover more ground because the work splits cleanly and nobody waits.

96
00:05:59.119 --> 00:06:02.720
That matters when the sub-tasks don't need to talk to each other.

97
00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.160
One thing to watch, the bill scales with the fan-out.

98
00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.600
And so does the wall clock because planning and merging

99
00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.160
both cost you before any answers get back.

100
00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:17.440
If work is coupled, you end up paying the tax and you don't get any benefit for it.

101
00:06:18.079 --> 00:06:19.600
That closes out this lesson.

102
00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:20.880
I'll see you in the next one.

